<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L12" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">These statements indicate that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEjOTlGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGLw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEjOTlGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGLw==</Equation> divided by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEjMTNGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGLw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEjMTNGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGLw==</Equation> results in a quotient of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiN0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiN0YnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation> and a remainder of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiOEYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiOEYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation>.  That is, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L13" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">These commands both accept a variable as an optional third argument.  In this case, the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:irem" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">iquo</Font></Hyperlink> command will still return the quotient but will assign the remainder to the name, while the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:irem" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">irem</Font></Hyperlink> command will return the remainder and assign the value of the quotient to the name.  Putting the name in right single quotes ensures that if the name already stores a value, it will be overwritten.  Omitting the single quotes will result in an error if the name has already been assigned a value.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L14" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">iquo(99,13,'remainderName');</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIo</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L15" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">remainderName;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIp</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L819" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Checking divisibility</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L17" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">To test whether one integer divides another, you check to see if the remainder is 0 or not.  For example, the following shows that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L18" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">irem(132,3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIh</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L20" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Since the question of whether one integer divides another is fundamental to our study of number theory, we'll write a procedure to test this for us.  The <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">IsDivisor</Font><Font encoding="UTF-8"> procedure written below accepts two integers as arguments.  It returns true if the first argument divides the second and false otherwise.  The procedure body is only one line \342\200\224 it computes the remainder and compares it to 0.</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L21" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">IsDivisor := proc(a::integer, b::integer)
  return evalb(irem(b,a) = 0);
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L22" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">IsDivisor(3,132);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L23" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">IsDivisor(13,99);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L821" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">The mod operator</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L26" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The textbook uses the notation <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to represent the remainder when <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is divided by <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Maple's <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mod</Font></Hyperlink> operator works in the same way.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L27" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">99 mod 13;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIp</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L29" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Recall from the division algorithm that the remainder must always be positive, even when the dividend is negative.  Maple's <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mod</Font></Hyperlink> function respects that convention by default.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L30" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">-27 mod 5;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L32" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">However, there may be times when it is more useful to allow negative values.  For example, consider the following question:  &quot;It is now 11:00 AM.  What time will it be 142 hours from now?&quot; If we compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>,</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L33" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">142 mod 24;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNB</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L823" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">we see that the time will be the same 142 hours from now as 22 hours from now.  But it's also the case that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, which means that the time 142 hours from now is the same as the time 2 hours earlier, <Font italic="true">i.e.</Font>, 9:00 AM.  You can see that this congruence is somewhat more convenient.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L35" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mods</Font></Hyperlink> command (the &quot;s&quot; is for symmetric) returns the integer closest to 0 that is congruent to the value in its first argument modulo the second argument.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L36" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">mods(142,24);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">ISIj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L38" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">modp</Font></Hyperlink> command returns the &quot;positive&quot; representation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L39" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">modp(142,24);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNB</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L803" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The default behavior of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mod</Font></Hyperlink> operator is to apply the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">modp</Font></Hyperlink> command to the operands.  However, this can be overridden by executing the assignment <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">`mod` := mods;</Font>, in which case <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mod</Font></Hyperlink> will act as the symmetric modulus.  Likewise, <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">`mod` := modp;</Font> will revert to the default.  Because of the possibility of modifying its<Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"></Hyperlink> behavior, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mod</Font></Hyperlink> is an ambiguous command and thus we will typically use the explicit <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">modp</Font></Hyperlink> command in procedures.  This way, there is no possibility that a reassignment of <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">`mod`</Font></Hyperlink> could wreck havoc on our programs.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L41" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Congruences</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L42" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The first argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">modp</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mods</Font></Hyperlink> can be any algebraic expression.  For example, you can compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L43" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">modp(3+4*9^2,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L45" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first argument can also be an equation.  Recall from Theorem 3 in Section 4.1 that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> if and only if <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  If you enter an equation as the first argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">modp</Font></Hyperlink>, Maple will evaluate both sides of the equation modulo the value given in the second argument.  For example, consider the congruence <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  In Maple, you would enter the following.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L46" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">modp(428=530,17);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">LyIiJEYj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L48" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that the result is the equation <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  By applying the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:evalb" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">evalb</Font></Hyperlink> command, we obtain a truth value.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L49" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(modp(428=530,17));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L50" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(modp(289=311,17));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L825" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Solving congruences</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L52" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple can solve congruences with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:msolve" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">msolve</Font></Hyperlink> command.  This command has two required arguments.  The first is an equation or set of equations representing the congruences to be solved.  The second argument is the modulus.  As an example, consider Exercise 13a from Section 4.1 of the textbook.  Under the assumption that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, we are to solve <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  We can solve this with Maple as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L53" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve({a=4,c=9*a},13);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCQvSSJhRzYiIiIlL0kiY0dGJSIjNQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L55" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If there are no solutions, then <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:msolve" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">msolve</Font></Hyperlink> will return NULL, so that no result is displayed.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L56" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve(n^2=3,4);</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L58" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">On the other hand, if the solution is indeterminate, then a family of solutions may be returned.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L59" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve(3^i=4,7);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">PCMvSSJpRzYiLCYiIiUiIiJJJF9aMUc2IiIiJw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L61" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The symbol <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUkjbWlHNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JVEkX1oxRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0Yn">LUkjbWlHNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JVEkX1oxRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0Yn</Equation> indicates that any integer may be substituted to obtain a value for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> that satisfies the congruence.  For example, substituting <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation> for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEkX1oxRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEkX1oxRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==</Equation> and then substituting the result into <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> yields</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L62" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">evalb(modp(3^(4+6*5)=4,7));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L64" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">If you prefer a particular name instead of symbols such as <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUkjbWlHNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JVEkX1oxRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0Yn">LUkjbWlHNiMvSSttb2R1bGVuYW1lRzYiSSxUeXBlc2V0dGluZ0dJKF9zeXNsaWJHRic2JVEkX1oxRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0Yn</Equation>, you can provide a name or set of names as an optional second argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:msolve" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">msolve</Font></Hyperlink> as illustrated below.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L65" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve(3^i=4,x,7);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">PCMvSSJpRzYiLCYiIiUiIiJJInhHNiIiIic=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L67" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" bold="true" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Arithmetic Modulo </Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2J1EibUYnLyUlYm9sZEdRJXRydWVGJy8lJ2l0YWxpY0dGMS8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1EsYm9sZC1pdGFsaWNGJy8lK2ZvbnR3ZWlnaHRHUSVib2xkRidGLy9GNUY5Rjc=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYmLUkjbWlHRiQ2J1EibUYnLyUlYm9sZEdRJXRydWVGJy8lJ2l0YWxpY0dGMS8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1EsYm9sZC1pdGFsaWNGJy8lK2ZvbnR3ZWlnaHRHUSVib2xkRidGLy9GNUY5Rjc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L68" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this subsection we'll define operators based on the definitions of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> given in the text.  Our goal will be to get as close as possible to being able to enter <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and have Maple return <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbW5HRiQ2JFEiNUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yv</Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L69" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The usual style of writing arithmetic operators in between the numbers being operated on is referred to as infix notation.  In Maple, we create operators that can be used in infix style by using <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:neutral" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">neutral operators</Font></Hyperlink>.  The name of a <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:neutral" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">neutral operator</Font></Hyperlink> must begin with an ampersand (&amp;) and be followed either by a valid Maple name composed of letters and numbers or by one or more allowable special characters, which include symbols such as + and *.  The two forms cannot be mixed, which means that if the name of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:neutral" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">neutral operator</Font></Hyperlink> includes a special character, then it cannot include letters or numbers.  We will use the names &amp;+ and &amp;* for our modular addition and multiplication operators.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L70" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You define a neutral operator in the same way as you normally define a procedure or functional operator, with some small differences.  First, when defining the operator, the name must be enclosed in left single quotes.  Second, the function or procedure should have only one or two arguments.  When there is one argument, the operator will function as a unary operator, like negation.  When two arguments are allowed, the operator will function like a binary operator such as addition.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L71" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We define addition and multiplication modulo 11 as follows.  The neutral operator names are assigned to functional operators which accept two arguments, <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">a</Font> and <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">b</Font>, and apply <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:mod" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">modp</Font></Hyperlink> to their sum or product, respectively.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L72" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`&amp;+` := (a,b) -&gt; modp(a + b,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Zio2JEkiYUc2IkkiYkdGJUYlNiRJKW9wZXJhdG9yR0YlSSZhcnJvd0dGJUYlLUklbW9kcEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2JCwmOSQiIiI5JUYwIiM2RiVGJUYl</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" labelreference="L73" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">`&amp;*` := (a,b) -&gt; modp(a * b,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">Zio2JEkiYUc2IkkiYkdGJUYlNiRJKW9wZXJhdG9yR0YlSSZhcnJvd0dGJUYlLUklbW9kcEclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc2JComOSQiIiI5JUYwIiM2RiVGJUYl</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L75" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">This allows us to perform arithmetic modulo 11 with infix notation.  (Note that these operators will not  respect the usual order of operations, so parentheses are needed.)</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L76" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">7 &amp;+ (9 &amp;* 2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L78" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Addition and Multiplication Tables</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L829" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We conclude this section by producing addition and multiplication tables.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L831" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We will represent the tables with matrices whose entries represent the sums or products.  In each matrix, the first row and first column should correspond to the value 0, so that the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> entry corresponds to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> entry to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, and so on.  In general, the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> entry should correspond to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L86" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Recall from Section 2.6 of this manual that you can define a matrix by specifying a size along with a procedure or function that accepts two arguments indicating the row and column position in the matrix and outputs the entry in that position.   As an example with <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L79" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L80" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L81" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Matrix(5,(i,j) -&gt; modp((i-1) + (j-1),5));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKz9uQzJW</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L82" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Matrix(5,(i,j) -&gt; modp((i-1) * (j-1),5));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LUknTWF0cml4RzYkJSpwcm90ZWN0ZWRHSShfc3lzbGliRzYiNiMvSSQlaWRHRiciKyUpXE5IVg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">4.2 Integer Representations and Algorithms</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L132" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section we will see how Maple can be used to explore representations of integers in various bases and algorithms for computing with integers.  We will begin by looking at Maple's built-in commands for converting between bases.  Then we'll focus our attention on binary representations of integers and the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Bits</Font></Hyperlink> package.  Finally, we'll see how to implement algorithms for addition and multiplication on binary representations.  In this section, we will restrict our attention to positive integers.  Many of the commands discussed here can be applied to negative integers as well, but we will not discuss their behavior in that case.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L133" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Base Conversion</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L134" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple provides support for converting from one base representation to another via the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">convert</Font></Hyperlink> command.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">convert</Font></Hyperlink> command has two required arguments.  The first argument is an expression that will be converted.  The second required argument is a &quot;form&quot; that specifies what the expression is converted into.  This command is extremely general and can be used to convert a variety of Maple objects into other objects.  As a first example, the command below converts the list provided as the first argument into a set.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L136" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert([1,2,3],set);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCUiIiIiIiMiIiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L138" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">There are over one hundred different valid forms available in Maple, and users can create additional forms in much the same way as types are created.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L139" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Maple includes forms for <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,binary" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">binary</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,octal" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">octal</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,hex" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">hexadecimal</Font></Hyperlink> (abbreviated <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,hex" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">hex</Font></Hyperlink>).  Using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">convert</Font></Hyperlink> command with a positive integer as the first argument and one of these forms as the second argument will produce the expected output.  Compare the following to Examples 4, 5, and 6 in the text.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L141" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(12345,octal);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiZyKyQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L142" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(177130,hex);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSYyQjNFQUc2Ig==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L143" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(241,binary);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiksKzY2</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L140" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To convert from a base other than ten to base ten, you use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,decimal" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">decimal</Font></Hyperlink> form together with a third argument indicating the base being converted from.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L145" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(30071,decimal,octal);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiZYQiI=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L146" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(11110001,decimal,binary);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRUIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L148" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For bases larger than 10 but less than 36, the characters &quot;A&quot; through &quot;Z&quot; (lowercase is also allowed) represent digits with values 10 or larger.  When letters are used as part of the representation, the entire representation must be enclosed in quotation marks.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L149" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(&quot;2B3EA&quot;,decimal,hex);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IidJcjw=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L151" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The third argument can also be given as an integer representing the base, which is particularly useful for converting from bases other than binary, octal, and hexadecimal.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L152" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(11110001,decimal,2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRUIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L153" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(12,decimal,3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIm</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L155" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The last example showed how to use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,decimal" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">convert</Font></Hyperlink> to go from a base 3 representation to a decimal representation.  We saw earlier how to convert from a decimal representation to representations in the common bases 2, 8, and 16.  Converting from a decimal representation to an arbitrary base requires a bit more explanation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L156" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Consider converting 194 in base ten to base five.  Using Algorithm 1, you can compute that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  To use Maple to obtain this representation, we use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">convert</Font></Hyperlink> command with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,base" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">base</Font></Hyperlink> form and a third argument representing the desired base.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L157" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(194,base,5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyYiIiUiIiQiIiMiIiI=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L833" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that this form does not return 1234.  Instead, it returns a list of the digits in &quot;reverse&quot; order, that is, with the least significant digit (that is, the &quot;one's&quot; place) first.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L159" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">This is the case even for the common bases, such as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,octal" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">octal</Font></Hyperlink>.  Also, by converting a decimal number to base 10, you can obtain the list of the digits.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L160" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(12345,base,8);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyciIiIiIigiIiFGJSIiJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L161" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert(12345,base,10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyciIiYiIiUiIiQiIiMiIiI=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L163" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,base" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">base</Font></Hyperlink> form can be used to convert between any two bases.  To do this, the first argument must be the list of digits in least significant first order.  The second argument is the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:convert,base" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">base</Font></Hyperlink> keyword.  The third argument is the original base, and the fourth argument is the target base.  For example, to find the base three expansion of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkobWZlbmNlZEdGJDYkLUYjNiQtSSNtbkdGJDYkUSQxMjNGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGN0Y3LUYjNiUtRjQ2JFEiNUYnRjcvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvRjhRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRidGNw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkobWZlbmNlZEdGJDYkLUYjNiQtSSNtbkdGJDYkUSQxMjNGJy8lLG1hdGh2YXJpYW50R1Enbm9ybWFsRidGN0Y3LUYjNiUtRjQ2JFEiNUYnRjcvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvRjhRJ2l0YWxpY0YnLyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRidGNw==</Equation> you would enter the following command.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L164" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">convert([3,2,1],base,5,3);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyYiIiMiIiEiIiJGJQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L166" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The result indicates that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L168" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Binary and the Bits Package</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L169" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We will now focus on binary representations.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Bits</Font></Hyperlink> package provides a selection of commands that are especially suited to working with binary representations.  In Chapter 2 of this manual, we used the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Bits</Font></Hyperlink> package to compute bitwise <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Here, we will make more extensive use of this package.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L835" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Split and Join</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L170" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Bits</Font></Hyperlink> package, the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Split" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Split</Font></Hyperlink> command is used to turn an integer into its binary representation as a list of 0s and 1s.  Note that once again the digits are listed in reverse order, <Font italic="true">i.e.</Font>, with the least significant digit first.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L171" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">with(Bits):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L172" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Split(241);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyoiIiIiIiFGJEYkRiNGI0YjRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L809" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The output above indicates that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L210" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Split" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Split</Font></Hyperlink> can accept the equation <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">bits=n</Font>, where <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">n</Font> is a positive integer, as an optional argument.  This specifies the number of bits to include in the result.  If <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">n</Font> is smaller than the number of bits needed to represent the integer, the bits in higher position are ignored.  If <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">n</Font> is larger than the minimum required number of bits, then 0s are added.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L211" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Split(241,bits=20);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NzYiIiIiIiFGJEYkRiNGI0YjRiNGJEYkRiRGJEYkRiRGJEYkRiRGJEYkRiQ=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L174" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The reverse operation is <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Join" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Join</Font></Hyperlink>, which accepts a list of digits in reverse order and returns the base ten representation of the integer.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L175" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Join([1,0,0,0,1,1,1,1]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRUIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L837" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">The GetBits command</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L179" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Related to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Split" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Split</Font></Hyperlink> is the more flexible <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,GetBits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">GetBits</Font></Hyperlink> command.  This command has two required arguments.  The first is an integer.  The second is a location, or a range, or a sequence of locations or ranges that you want to extract.  The least significant digit is considered to have location 0, while -1 can be used to refer to the most significant digit.  Note that <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:GetBits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">GetBits</Font></Hyperlink> returns a sequence, so it must be enclosed in brackets to obtain a list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L180" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[GetBits(241,0..-1)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyoiIiIiIiFGJEYkRiNGI0YjRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L181" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[GetBits(241,0..3)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyYiIiIiIiFGJEYk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L182" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[GetBits(241,0,4..-1)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyciIiJGI0YjRiNGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L811" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note in the last example, the command requested the location <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">0</Font> followed by the range <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">4..-1</Font>.  This selected all of the 1 digits from the binary representation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L184" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The example below uses the range <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">-1..0</Font> to output the digits in the usual order.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L185" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[GetBits(241,-1..0)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyoiIiJGI0YjRiMiIiFGJEYkRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L187" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can also pass the equation <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">output=number</Font> as an optional argument.  This causes <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,GetBits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">GetBits</Font></Hyperlink> to output the result as a decimal number.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L188" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[GetBits(241,1..4)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyYiIiFGI0YjIiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L189" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">GetBits(241,1..4,output=number);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIp</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L839" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Bitwise operations</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L841" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Finally, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Bits</Font></Hyperlink> contains several commands for performing bitwise logical operations: <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Not" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Not</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">And</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Or" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Or</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Xor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Xor</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Nand" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Nand</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Nor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Nor</Font></Hyperlink>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Iff" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Iff</Font></Hyperlink>, and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Implies" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Implies</Font></Hyperlink>.  Each of these can be applied to two integers (excepting <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,Not" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Not</Font></Hyperlink>) and returns the integer obtained as the result of applying the bitwise operation to the binary representations of the integers.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L191" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For example, consider <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L192" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Split(43);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NygiIiJGIyIiIUYjRiRGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L193" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Split(44);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NygiIiFGIyIiIkYkRiNGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L195" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Applying <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to each pair of corresponding digits produces 0,0,0,1,0,1.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L196" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">zip(And,Split(43),Split(44));</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NygiIiFGI0YjIiIiRiNGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L198" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This corresponds to the integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L199" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Join([0,0,0,1,0,1]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNT</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L201" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This is the same result as you get from applying <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:Bits,And" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">And</Font></Hyperlink> to 43 and 44.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L202" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">And(43,44);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNT</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L204" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Binary Addition</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L213" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this subsection, we will implement Algorithm 2 from Section 4.2, addition of integers.  Our procedure will accept two binary representations (lists of 0s and 1s with the least significant digit first).  The first task for our procedure will be to make sure that the binary representations are of the same length.  To do this, we compute the maximum of the lengths of the two lists (stored as <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>) and then add as many 0s to the list as are necessary to make both lists that length.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L214" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Next, we initialize a sum list <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> to a list of all 0s.  The sum <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiU0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> must have size <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to allow for a carry to surpass the lengths of the two input values.  Once these initial tasks are completed, we follow Algorithm 2.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L215" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Addition := proc(a::list({0,1}), b::list({0,1}))
  local n, A, B, S, c, j, d;
  n := max(nops(a),nops(b));
  A := [op(a), 0 $ (n-nops(a))];
  B := [op(b), 0 $ (n-nops(b))];
  S := [0 $ n+1];
  c := 0;
  for j from 1 to n do
    d := floor((A[j]+B[j]+c)/2);
    S[j] := A[j] + B[j] + c - 2*d;
    c := d;
  end do;
  S[n+1] := c;
  return S;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L217" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Adding <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> with our procedure produces:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L218" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Addition([0,1,0,1],[0,1,0,1,1,1]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NykiIiFGIyIiIkYjRiNGI0Yk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L219" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Join(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiNv</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L221" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Binary Multiplication</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L222" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Finally, we will implement a multiplication algorithm, presented as Algorithm 3 in Section 4.2.  Once again, our procedure will accept the binary representations of positive integers as the inputs.  This time, however, it is not necessary for them to have the same length.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L225" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The shift that occurs when <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> will be accomplished as follows.  To shift the list <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">[1,1,1,1]</Font> by 5 places, we must prepend 5 zeros on front of the list.  (Remember, our binary representations have least significant digit first, which is why 0s are added to the front of the list instead of the back.)  We do this by creating a new list that begins with 5 zeros and is followed by the elements of the original list.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L226" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">shiftExample := [1,1,1,1];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">NyYiIiJGI0YjRiM=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L227" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">[0 $ 5, op(shiftExample)];</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NysiIiFGI0YjRiNGIyIiIkYkRiRGJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L229" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note the use of the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:dollar" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">$</Font></Hyperlink> operator to repeat the 0 five times.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L223" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We will store the partial products as a <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:table" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">table</Font></Hyperlink> of lists.  Recall from Section 2.3 of this manual that we can create a <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:table" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">table</Font></Hyperlink> by assigning <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">table()</Font> to a name.  We can then use the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:selection" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Text" underline="true" foreground="[0,128,128]">selection operation</Font></Hyperlink> (brackets) to both assign entries to indices and to retrieve entries.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L224" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The product <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> will be initialized to [0], a binary representation of 0.  The addition in the final for loop will be performed by the <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">Addition</Font> procedure we created above.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L230" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here is our implementation of Algorithm 3.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L231" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Multiplication := proc(a::list({0,1}), b::list({0,1}))
  local j, C, p;
  C := table();
  for j from 1 to nops(b) do
    if b[j] = 1 then
       C[j] := [ 0 $ (j-1), op(a) ];
    else
       C[j] := [0];
    end if;
  end do;
  p := [0];
  for j from 1 to nops(b) do
    p := Addition(p,C[j]);
  end do;
  return p;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L233" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We test our procedure using Example 10 from Section 4.2.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L234" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">Multiplication([0,1,1],[1,0,1]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NygiIiEiIiJGJEYkRiRGIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1042" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">4.3 Primes and Greatest Common Divisors</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L237" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section we will see how to use Maple to find primes, find prime factorizations, and compute greatest common divisors and least common multiples.  We will also use Maple's capabilities to explore the distribution of primes.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L238" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Primes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L843" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We will first introduce some of Maple's commands for testing whether a number is prime and for finding primes.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L845" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Testing for primality</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L847" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> command accepts a single argument, an integer to be tested, and returns true or false.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L240" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">isprime(5);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L241" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">isprime(10);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L242" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">isprime(2^13-1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L848" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Unlike the trial division algorithm discussed in the book, which checks all possible divisors to see if a number is prime or composite, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> uses a probabilistic primality test.  This probabilistic test gains much faster performance at the cost of a small possibility that the command will return an incorrect result.  As the help page asserts, there is no known example of an integer for which <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> is incorrect and any such example must be exceptionally large.  So, despite there being a chance of error, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> is in fact very reliable. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L851" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Listing primes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L244" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ithprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ithprime</Font></Hyperlink> accepts as input a positive integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and computes the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>th prime number. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L245" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ithprime(1);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L246" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ithprime(2);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L247" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">seq(ithprime(i),i=1..20);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NjYiIiMiIiQiIiYiIigiIzYiIzgiIzwiIz4iI0IiI0giI0oiI1AiI1QiI1YiI1oiI2AiI2YiI2giI24iI3I=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L248" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ithprime(100000);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">Iig0KCpIIg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L250" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">While <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> has an (extremely) small possibility of being in error, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ithprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ithprime</Font></Hyperlink>'s outputs are guaranteed to be prime.  For small prime numbers, it simply looks up the result in an internal table, while for larger arguments, it operates recursively.  This function should be used when an application needs to be certain of the primality of an integer and when speed is not an overriding concern.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L251" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Maple also provides the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">nextprime</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">prevprime</Font></Hyperlink> commands.  Both commands accept an integer as their single argument.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">nextprime</Font></Hyperlink> command returns the smallest prime larger than the input value, and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">prevprime</Font></Hyperlink> returns the largest prime smaller than the input.  For example, to find the first prime number larger than 1000, we enter the following.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L252" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">nextprime(1000);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiU0NQ==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L254" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Similarly, the prime number before that one is</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L255" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">prevprime(%);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQoKio=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L257" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that both <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">nextprime</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">prevprime</Font></Hyperlink> are based on the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> command, so their results are subject to the same tiny possibility of error resulting from the probabilistic test used by <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L853" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Inspecting procedure definitions</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L258" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We can see that <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:nextprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">nextprime</Font></Hyperlink> relies on <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> by looking at its definition.  To do this, we set the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:interface" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">interface</Font></Hyperlink> variable <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">verboseproc</Font> equal to 2 and then call <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:eval" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">eval</Font></Hyperlink> on the procedure name, as below.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L259" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">interface(verboseproc=2):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L260" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">eval(nextprime);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L262" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">verboseproc</Font> variable controls how much detail is shown about procedures.  A value of 2 forces printing the full body for all procedures.  The default value of 1 prints the body for user-defined procedures only.  We reset the value back to the default.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L263" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">interface(verboseproc=1):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L265" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Prime Factorization</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L266" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">To compute the prime factorization of an integer, we can use the Maple command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactor</Font></Hyperlink>.  For example, we can factor 100 using <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactor</Font></Hyperlink> as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L267" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactor(100);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KiYtSSFHNiI2IyIiI0YnLUYkNiMiIiZGJw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L268" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactor(123456789);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KigtSSFHNiI2IyIiJCIiIy1GJDYjIiUuUSIiIi1GJDYjIiUyT0Ys</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L269" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactor(-987654321);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">LCQqKC1JIUc2IjYjIiIkIiIjLUYlNiMiIzxGKS1GJTYjIidAKHokIiIiISIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L854" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The &quot;i&quot; in <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactor</Font></Hyperlink> refers to integer factorization.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:factor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">factor</Font></Hyperlink> command is used for factoring polynomials.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L288" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:expand" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">expand</Font></Hyperlink> command can be used to reverse the process.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L289" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">expand(<Label label="L269" view="label" executable="true"></Label>);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">ISpAVmwoKSo=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L271" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactor</Font></Hyperlink> can accept optional arguments that allow you to specify the method Maple uses to factor the integer.  A discussion of these methods is beyond the scope of this manual.  However, one method, the &quot;<Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">easy</Font>&quot; method is worth mentioning.  The following example illustrates the effect of the <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">easy</Font> method.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L272" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactor(236914830635411777378758175934586404476822);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KiwtSSFHNiI2IyIiIyIiIi1GJDYjIiQoPkYoLUYkNiMiJDQmRigtRiQ2IyIpaClIQCRGJy1GJDYjIilCKGYvIiIiJA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L273" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactor(236914830635411777378758175934586404476822,easy);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output" display="">KiotSSFHNiI2IyIiIyIiIi1GJDYjIiQoPkYoLUYkNiMiJDQmRihJJ19jMzdfMUdGJUYo</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L277" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first command above factors the given integer into its complete prime factorization.  The second produces the &quot;easy&quot; factors, with the symbol <Font style="2D Output" italic="true">_c37_1</Font> indicating that the remaining factor has 37 digits.  This gives you a way to have Maple only perform the easy parts of factorizations, which can help ensure that your procedures run quickly during development. Then, when you are ready to let the procedure take all the time it needs, you can remove the <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">easy</Font> keyword.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L282" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactors" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactors</Font></Hyperlink> command is different from the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactor</Font></Hyperlink> command.  Instead of producing an algebraic expression of the form <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactors" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactors</Font></Hyperlink> produces a list of the form <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, where the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImlGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImlGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> are the prime factors, the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiZUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImlGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiZUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImlGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> their multiplicities, and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is the sign of the integer, represented as positive or negative 1.  Compare the output below to the results from <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactor" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactor</Font></Hyperlink> at the start of this subsection.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L283" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactors(100);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyQiIiI3JDckIiIjRiY3JCIiJkYm</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L284" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactors(123456789);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyQiIiI3JTckIiIkIiIjNyQiJS5RRiM3JCIlMk9GIw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L285" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ifactors(-987654321);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NyQhIiI3JTckIiIkIiIjNyQiIzxGJzckIidAKHokIiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L287" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The format returned by <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ifactors" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ifactors</Font></Hyperlink> can be easier to use in programs.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L290" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">The Distribution of Primes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L291" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The Prime Number Theorem (Theorem 4 in Section 4.3 of the text) tells us that the number of primes not exceeding <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is approximated by the function <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  In this subsection, we will use Maple's graphing capabilities to graph the number of primes not exceeding <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L292" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Recall from Section 3.3 of this manual that we can graph specific points by using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:plot" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">plot</Font></Hyperlink> command applied to two lists where the first list is the list of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-values and the second list is the list of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>-values.  We will consider the integers from 1 to 1000, so our first list is obtained as follows (the output has been suppressed).</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L293" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">xList := [ $ 1..1000 ]:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L295" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">To find the number of primes not exceeding <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, we use the command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory,pi" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">pi</Font></Hyperlink> found in the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">numtheory</Font></Hyperlink> package.  The function <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==</Equation> is the standard notation for the number of primes less than or equal to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  To calculate the number of primes less than or equal to 1000, for example, we enter the following.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L296" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">numtheory[pi](1000);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiRvIg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L855" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that the number <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yy">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJ0Yy</Equation>, the ratio of the circumference of a circle to its diameter, is denoted <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:initialconstants" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">Pi</Font></Hyperlink> in Maple.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L298" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We obtain the list of values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==</Equation> associated to the values of <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">xList</Font> by</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L299" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">piList := [seq(numtheory[pi](x),x=1..1000)]:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L301" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">As we did in the solution to Computer Project 9 of Chapter 3, we are going to graphically compare the values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==</Equation> to the function <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  We will define two plot objects and then combine them with the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:plots,display" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">display</Font></Hyperlink> command.  Refer to Chapter 3 of this manual, particularly section 3.3 and the solution to Computer Project 9, for detailed information about the commands used here.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L302" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">piPlot := plot(xList,piList,color=blue,view=[1..1000,0..170],style=point,symbol=solidcircle,symbolsize=7,legend=&quot;pi(x)&quot;):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L303" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">xlnxPlot := plot(x/ln(x),x=1..1000,color=red,view=[1..1000,0..25],legend=&quot;x/ln(x)&quot;):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L304" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">plots[display](piPlot,xlnxPlot);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="Maple Plot" layout="Maple Plot"><Plot height="236" type="two-dimensional" width="236" gridlinevisibility="1" legendvisibility="true"></Plot></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L316" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Notice that while the blue line representing <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEnJiM5NjA7RicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSZmYWxzZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdub3JtYWxGJy1JKG1mZW5jZWRHRiQ2JC1GIzYkLUYsNiVRInhGJy9GMFEldHJ1ZUYnL0YzUSdpdGFsaWNGJ0YyRjJGMg==</Equation> seems to remain above the red line representing <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, it is fairly clear from the graph that they grow at the same rate.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L317" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Greatest Common Divisors and Least Common Multiples</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L318" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple provides commands <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcd</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ilcm" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ilcm</Font></Hyperlink> for computing the greatest common divisor and the least common multiple of integers.  To compute the greatest common divisor of two integers, you apply the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcd</Font></Hyperlink> command to them.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L319" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">igcd(6,9);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L321" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">You can also compute the greatest common divisor of more than two integers.  For more than 2 integers, the greatest common divisor is defined to be the largest integer that is a divisor of all of the integers.  So for example, 3 divides 6, 9, and 12, so </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L322" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">igcd(6,9,12);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIk</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L940" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ilcm" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ilcm</Font></Hyperlink> command finds the least common multiple of two or more integers.  For example,</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L939" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ilcm(6,9);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM9</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L938" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ilcm(12,18,33);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiQnUg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L937" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that Maple also includes commands called <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:gcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">gcd</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:gcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">lcm</Font></Hyperlink>.  These commands are more general than <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcd</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ilcm" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ilcm</Font></Hyperlink>, and can find the greatest common divisor and least common multiple of polynomials as well as integers.  The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcd</Font></Hyperlink> and <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:ilcm" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">ilcm</Font></Hyperlink> commands, however, are optimized for integer inputs and should generally be the commands used when working with integers.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L927" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Relatively prime</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L929" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Recall from the text that two numbers are said to be relatively prime if their greatest common divisor is 1.  For example, consider 10 and 21.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L930" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">igcd(10,21);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L931" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Since <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcd</Font></Hyperlink> returned 1, we conclude that 10 and 21 are relatively prime.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L324" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The following procedure accepts two integers as input and returns true if they are relatively prime and false otherwise.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L325" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AreRelPrime := proc(a::integer, b::integer)
  if gcd(a,b) = 1 then
    return true;
  else
    return false;
  end if;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L326" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AreRelPrime(3,6);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L327" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">AreRelPrime(22,15);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSV0cnVlRyUqcHJvdGVjdGVkRw==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L933" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Pairwise relatively prime</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L935" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Also recall that a list of integers <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is said to be pairwise relatively prime if <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> whenever <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  That is, when every pair is relatively prime.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L329" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">We can write a procedure to test whether or not a list of integers is pairwise relatively prime as follows.  We use two for loops with variables <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  The first loop, the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> loop, runs from 1 to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> (<Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> cannot be equal to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> because then <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> would have to be strictly greater than <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and less than or equal to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>).  The inner loop has <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> run from <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> so that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is always greater than <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Within the two loops, we test the greatest common divisor of the entries in the input list with indices <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiaUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiakYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  If the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEkZ2NkRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEkZ2NkRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==</Equation> is ever not 1, the procedure immediately returns false.  If all the pairs pass the test, then after both loops are terminated, true is returned.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L330" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ArePairwisePrime := proc(A::list(integer))
  local n, i, j;
  n := nops(A);
  for i from 1 to n-1 do
    for j from i+1 to n do
      if gcd(A[i],A[j]) &lt;&gt; 1 then
        return false;
      end if;
    end do;
  end do;
  return true;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L332" drawlabel="true">
