<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L335" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">However, 42, 165, and 182 are not pairwise relatively prime, although their common <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEkZ2NkRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEkZ2NkRicvJSdpdGFsaWNHUSV0cnVlRicvJSxtYXRodmFyaWFudEdRJ2l0YWxpY0YnL0YzUSdub3JtYWxGJw==</Equation> is 1.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L336" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">igcd(42,165,182);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L337" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">ArePairwisePrime([42,165,182]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">SSZmYWxzZUclKnByb3RlY3RlZEc=</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L339" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">The extended Euclidean algorithm</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L344" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">While <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcd" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcd</Font></Hyperlink> is useful for calculating the greatest common divisor of integers, it is also useful to be able to express the greatest common divisor as an integral combination of the integers.  Specifically, given integers <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, we may wish to express <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> as <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> where <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEidEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEidEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation><Font encoding="UTF-8"> are integers.  The fact that such integers always exist is known as B\303\251zout's Theorem, given in the text as Theorem 6 of Section 4.3.  In the preamble to Exercise 41 in the text, the extended Euclidean algorithm is described, which produces not only the greatest common divisor but also the integers </Font><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEidEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEidEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L345" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">In Maple, the command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcdex" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcdex</Font></Hyperlink> is an implementation of the extended Euclidean algorithm.  This command accepts two integers and two optional names.  When executed, Maple returns the greatest common divisor of the two integers.  If the optional names have been included, then the values of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEidEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEidEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> are stored in them.  As an example, consider 252 and 198, the values used in Example 17.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L346" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">igcdex(252,198,'s','t');</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiM9</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L347" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">s; t;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIl</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">ISIm</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L349" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The results above indicate that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  We enclose the names <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">s</Font> and <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">t</Font> in single right quotes to ensure that we pass their names rather than any previously assigned values.  If the quotes were not present and one of the names had previously been assigned a value, an error would result.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L1043" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block>
</Section>
<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 2" layout="Heading 2">4.4 Solving Congruences</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L353" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In this section, we will see how Maple can be used to solve congruences.  We will begin the section by looking at how to find inverses and solve linear congruences.  We will then consider the Chinese Remainder Theorem.  Next, we will use Maple to find pseudoprimes, and we conclude with an exploration of primitive roots and discrete logarithms.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L354" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Modular Inverses</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L357" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"><Font encoding="UTF-8">Example 1 of Section 4.4 of the text demonstrates how B\303\251zout coefficients can be used to find the inverse of an integer modulo a number.  In the previous section of this manual, we saw that the </Font><Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcdex" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcdex</Font></Hyperlink><Font encoding="UTF-8"> command can be used to obtain the B\303\251zout coefficients.  </Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L943" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Finding inverses with igcdex</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L355" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">For example, to find the inverse of 264 modulo 3185, we need to find <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEic0YnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> so that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> (provided that 264 and 3185 are relatively prime).   </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L358" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">igcdex(264,3185,'s','t');</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L945" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Since the statement returned 1, we know that 264 and 3185 are relatively prime.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L360" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Hyperlink linktarget="Help:igcdex" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">igcdex</Font></Hyperlink> assigns the coefficient of the first integer to the first name and the second number to the second name. </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L361" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">s;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiR1JA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L362" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">t;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">ISNK</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L364" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">This indicates that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  And thus 374 is the inverse of 264 modulo 3185.  We can confirm this by computing the product modulo 3185.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L365" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">374*264 mod 3185;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIi</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L947" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Finding inverses with ^(-1)</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L949" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple actually provides a simpler way to compute the modular inverse.  The textbook uses the notation <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> to indicate the modular inverse of an integer.  An alternate notation is <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, which calls to mind the notation used in algebra for reciprocals, as in <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  Maple interprets an exponent of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, within the context of modular arithmetic, as the modular inverse.  For example, we can obtain the inverse of 264 modulo 3185 as follows.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L367" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal"></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L368" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">264^(-1) mod 3185;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiR1JA==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L370" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Note that the parentheses around <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> are required.  Also note that if the integer and the modulus are not relatively prime, no inverse exists and an error is generated.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L371" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">4^(-1) mod 10;</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="HyperlinkError" layout="HyperlinkError"><Hyperlink linktarget="http://www.maplesoft.com/support/help/errors/view.aspx?path=Error,%20the%20modular%20inverse%20does%20not%20exist" hyperlink="true"><Font style="HyperlinkError">Error, the modular inverse does not exist</Font></Hyperlink></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L952" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Solving congruences</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L373" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We saw in Section 4.1 of this manual the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:msolve" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">msolve</Font></Hyperlink> command for solving congruences.  We can use this command to solve linear congruences of the form <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> as follows.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L374" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve(4*x=3,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">PCMvSSJ4RzYiIiIq</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L954" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The first argument to <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:msolve" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">msolve</Font></Hyperlink> is the congruence expressed with an equals sign and the second is the modulus.  Maple returns a set whose elements express the solution to the congruence.  If there is no solution, Maple returns nothing (technically, the command returns <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">NULL</Font>, which results in no output being displayed).  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L376" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The following attempts to solve <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>, which is the same as finding an inverse for 4 modulo 10 and has no solution.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L377" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve(4*x=1,10);</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L379" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">It is also possible to have multiple solutions.  For example, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L380" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">msolve(3*x=9,12);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NiU8Iy9JInhHNiIiIiQ8Iy9GJSIiKDwjL0YlIiM2</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L382" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The reader should refer back to Section 4.1 of this manual for information about solving systems of congruences with the same modulus.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L383" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">The Chinese Remainder Theorem</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L384" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The text describes two approaches to solving systems of congruences of the form</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L399" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L400" drawlabel="true">
