<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L389" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The first approach, used in Example 5 of Section 4.4, is based on the proof of the Chinese Remainder Theorem.  The second approach is the technique of back substitution described in Example 6.  Maple's command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:chrem" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">chrem</Font></Hyperlink> is an efficient implementation of back substitution.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L956" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:chrem" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">chrem</Font></Hyperlink> command accepts two lists as its arguments.  The first argument is the list <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> and the second is the list of moduli <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  The result is the smallest positive integer that satisfies all of the congruences.  As an example, we solve the congruences </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L958" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
