<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiVhNg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L964" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Creating our own procedure</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L408" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">In the remainder of this subsection we will provide an implementation of the method for solving systems of congruences.  This implementation will be based on the construction given in the proof of the Chinese Remainder Theorem.  While this will be less efficient than Maple's <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:chrem" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">chrem</Font></Hyperlink> command, implementing the algorithm can help you to better understand the proof of the theorem.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L409" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Our procedure, which we call <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">CRTheorem</Font>, will accept as input two lists, <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">a</Font> and <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">m</Font>, representing the values and the moduli of the congruences.  It will begin with two tests to check that the lists are the same length and that the moduli are in fact pairwise relatively prime, as is required by the assumptions of the theorem.  We use <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">ArePairwisePrime</Font> from Section 4.3 of this manual to check that the moduli are pairwise relatively prime.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L410" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Once the preliminary tests are complete, the procedure sets <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> equal to the product of the moduli.  (Note that P corresponds to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> in the statement of the theorem in the text.  This is the only notational difference between our procedure and the text.)  Recall that the Maple command <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mul</Font></Hyperlink> computes the product of the values obtained by evaluating the first argument at each element in the range given in the second argument.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L411" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The procedure then needs to compute the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiTUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiTUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> and <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation>.  To do this, we'll create empty tables <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">M</Font> and <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">y</Font> to store the values.  Once the empty tables are initialized, the procedure enters a for loop in which the values for <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">M</Font> and <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">y</Font> are calculated.  The values for <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">M</Font> are calculated by the formula <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  For y, we use the fact that the <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEieUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> are the inverses of <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiTUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiTUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation> modulo <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUklbXN1YkdGJDYlLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibUYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy1GIzYlLUYvNiVRImtGJ0YyRjVGMkY1LyUvc3Vic2NyaXB0c2hpZnRHUSIwRicvRjZRJ25vcm1hbEYn</Equation>, that is, <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L414" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Finally, we compute the result <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> using the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:add" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">add</Font></Hyperlink> command and return the result modulo <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiUEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>.  Here is the procedure.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L415" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CRTheorem := proc(a::list(integer),m::list(posint))
  local P, M, y, i, x;
  if not nops(a) = nops(m) then
    error &quot;Lists must be the same length.&quot;;
  end if;
  if not ArePairwisePrime(m) then
    error &quot;Moduli must be pairwise relatively prime.&quot;;
  end if;
  P := mul(m[i],i=1..nops(m));
  M := table();
  y := table();
  for i from 1 to nops(m) do
    M[i] := P/m[i];
    y[i] := M[i]^(-1) mod m[i];
  end do;
  x := add(a[i]*M[i]*y[i],i=1..nops(m));
  return x mod P;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L417" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that our procedure produces the same result as <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:chrem" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">chrem</Font></Hyperlink> did above.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L418" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CRTheorem([2,4,6,10],[3,5,7,11]);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiVhNg==</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L420" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Pseudoprimes</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L421" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Recall from the text that a pseudoprime to the base <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> is a composite number <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> such that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  We will write a procedure to find pseudoprimes.  Our procedure will accept two arguments, the base <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEiYkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> and a maximum value for <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation>, and will return a list of the pseudoprimes that it identifies.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L423" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The algorithm is fairly straightforward.  We will use a for loop beginning at 3, ending with the specified maximum and increasing by 2 each time (so as to skip even integers).  Within the loop, we first test the congruence.  If the congruence holds, then we use <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:isprime" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">isprime</Font></Hyperlink> to check whether the number is prime or composite.  If it is composite, then it is added to the list of pseudoprimes.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L424" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">FindPseudoprimes := proc(b::posint, max::posint)
  local PList, n;
  PList := [];
  for n from 3 to max by 2 do
    if (b &amp;^ (n-1) mod n) = 1 then
      if not isprime(n) then
        PList := [op(PList),n];
      end if;
    end if;
  end do;
  return PList;
end proc: </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L426" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Note that we used <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">&amp;^</Font> in the calculation of the congruence instead of <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">^</Font>.  The syntax <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">&amp;^</Font> is the &quot;inert&quot; version of the exponent operator.  When we use <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">^</Font>, the integer exponentiation is performed first, which can result in an extremely large number.  With the inert <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">&amp;^</Font> operator, Maple performs the exponentiation intelligently, using techniques such as those discussed in Section 4.2 of the text for performing efficient modular exponentiation.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L427" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Here are the pseudoprimes to the base 2 up to <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L428" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">FindPseudoprimes(2,100000);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output"></Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L393" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font bold="true">Primitive Roots and Discrete Logarithms</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L445" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple includes several commands for computing primitive roots and discrete logarithms.  The commands we will discuss here are found in the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">numtheory</Font></Hyperlink> package.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L446" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">with(numtheory):</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L979" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10"><Font italic="true">Primitive roots</Font></Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L440" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Maple provides a command, <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory,primroot" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">primroot</Font></Hyperlink>, that computes primitive roots.  There are two forms of this command.  The first takes one argument, the modulus, and returns the smallest primitive root.  For example, the smallest primitive root of 11 is 2.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L441" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">primroot(11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIj</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L447" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">The second form of <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory,primroot" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">primroot</Font></Hyperlink> takes two arguments.  The first argument is a nonnegative integer and the second is the modulus.  The command returns the smallest primitive root of the modulus that is greater than the first argument.  For example, the next smallest primitive roots are</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L448" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">primroot(2,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIn</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L449" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">primroot(6,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIo</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L450" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">primroot(7,11);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">IiIp</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group>
<Group labelreference="L451" drawlabel="true">
