<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L580" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">We conclude this section with a procedure to check the validity of an ISBN.  Recall that the ISBN-10 code consists of 10 digits, the last of which is computed by the formula </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L581" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" alignment="centred"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L582" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The symbol X is used in case <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L583" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">Our <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">checkISBN</Font> procedure will accept the ISBN as a string.  It is necessary that we use strings in case the ISBN contains X as the check digit.  Consider the ISBN</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L584" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
