<Section collapsed="false" MultipleChoiceAnswerIndex="-1" MultipleChoiceRandomizeChoices="false" TrueFalseAnswerIndex="-1" EssayAnswerRows="5" EssayAnswerColumns="60"><Title>
<Text-field style="Heading 3" layout="Heading 3">Computations and Explorations 1</Text-field></Title><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L756" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spacebelow="10" leftmargin="10" spaceabove="10">Determine whether <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is prime for each of the primes not exceeding 100.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L757" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">Solution: To solve this problem, we will write a Maple program that tests each prime <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> less than or equal to a given value to see whether <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is a Mersenne prime.  The procedure will output a list of those primes <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> for which <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is prime.  </Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group autoexecute="true" view="code" labelreference="L758" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CheckMersenne := proc(max::posint)
  local p, L;
  p := 2;
  L := [];
  while p &lt;= max do
    if isprime(2^p-1) then
      L := [op(L),p];
    end if;
    p := nextprime(p);
  end do;
  return L;
end proc:</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L760" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal">The primes <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEicEYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> less than 100 such that <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is prime are</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
<Group view="code" labelreference="L761" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field prompt="&gt; " style="Maple Input" layout="Normal">CheckMersenne(100);</Text-field>
</Input>
<Output>
<Text-field style="2D Output" layout="Maple Output"><Equation executable="false" style="2D Output">NywiIiMiIiQiIiYiIigiIzgiIzwiIz4iI0oiI2giIyop</Equation></Text-field>
</Output>
</Group></Presentation-Block><Presentation-Block>
<Group view="presentation" hide-input="false" hide-output="true" inline-output="false" labelreference="L763" drawlabel="true">
<Input>
<Text-field style="Text" layout="Normal" spaceabove="10">For another approach, consider the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory,mersenne" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mersenne</Font></Hyperlink> command from the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">numtheory</Font></Hyperlink> package.  This command is based on a table lookup.  The command accepts one argument, either an integer or a list with one integer element.  If you pass an integer <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display="LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=">LUklbXJvd0c2Iy9JK21vZHVsZW5hbWVHNiJJLFR5cGVzZXR0aW5nR0koX3N5c2xpYkdGJzYkLUkjbWlHRiQ2JVEibkYnLyUnaXRhbGljR1EldHJ1ZUYnLyUsbWF0aHZhcmlhbnRHUSdpdGFsaWNGJy9GM1Enbm9ybWFsRic=</Equation> to the <Hyperlink linktarget="Help:numtheory,mersenne" hyperlink="true"><Font style="Maple Input" underline="true" executable="false">mersenne</Font></Hyperlink> command, it will compute <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation>.  If that value is prime, the command returns it.  It returns false if <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is composite.  And if it cannot determine whether <Equation executable="false" style="2D Math" input-equation="" display=""></Equation> is prime or not, it returns <Font style="Maple Input" executable="false">FAIL</Font>.</Text-field>
</Input>
</Group>
